\description{
Counts the rows without f0 in each token: at the start of the token,
at its end, and inside it, in gaps between two samples with f0. It also
counts the gaps that the time steps show, and the short runs of samples
that a gap cuts off at the start or end of a token. Nothing is changed:
\code{\link[=drop_rows_without_f0]{drop_rows_without_f0()}} removes the rows, and the counts here say what it
does with the same arguments. The app's Start page shows these counts when
a file is uploaded.
}
\details{
\strong{Rows without f0.} A row has no f0 when its value in the \code{f0} column is
\code{NA}, \code{NaN} or infinite, or is one of the \code{unvoiced} values. Only this one
column counts. Run \code{\link[=zero_hz_to_na]{zero_hz_to_na()}} first, so that 0 Hz counts as no f0
in Hz columns. Other 0 and negative values count as f0 unless they are
listed in \code{unvoiced}: in a signed or normalised column (semitones,
z-scores) they are real values.

\strong{Where they are.} Rows are taken in time order within each token,
whatever their order in \code{data}. A row without f0 is at the start when its
time is before the token's first sample with f0, at the end when it is
after the last one, and inside otherwise. A row without f0 at the same
time as a sample with f0 counts as inside, but it makes no gap. A token
with no f0 at all is counted apart: its rows are none of start, end or
inside. A row with no time (\code{NA}) cannot be placed either. It still counts
in \code{n_rows}, and in \code{n_f0} when it has f0. A row whose token is \code{NA}
belongs to a token of its own.

\strong{Gaps.} A gap is a stretch inside a token with no f0. A gap from rows has
one or more rows without f0 between two samples with f0. A gap from time
steps is a step between two samples with f0 that is more than \code{gap_ratio}
times the token's usual step. Gaps from time steps are read only when the
time steps form a regular grid. Then the same gaps show whether or not the
rows without f0 are still in the data, for tokens whose usual step is one
frame. (In a token where every other frame has no f0, the usual step is
two frames, so once those rows are removed the steps show no gap.) The
attribute \code{grid} says whether the grid is regular, and why not. Samples
timed at each glottal pulse are not regular. Give the real time column,
never a \verb{*_t01}, \verb{*_tseq} or \verb{*_tbound} axis: those restart or change
step at every segment.

The default \code{gap_ratio = 1.5} already means "one frame or more missing":
a gap of one frame doubles the step, and 1.5 leaves room for jitter and
floating-point noise in the times.

\strong{Times written to the step.} When the times are written about as
finely as the step, as for a 10 ms grid written to 2 decimals (0.01, 0.02,
...), the steps are read in whole units of 0.01: a step of 0.02 or more is
a gap. This needs at least 99\% of the steps to be exactly one unit. A
true step that is not a whole number of units fails this, such as 12.5 ms
or 7.5 ms written to 2 decimals: rounding gives steps of 0 or 0.02 in a
regular pattern, not gaps. So does a file with more than 1 gap in 100
steps, as the two cannot be told apart. Times written more coarsely than
the step, such as a 25 ms grid written to 2 decimals, give no gaps from
the time steps. A true step within 1\% of the unit, such as 10.05 ms
written to 2 decimals, gives too few 2-unit steps to be told from real
gaps, so write the times to at least 3 decimals when the step is not
exactly 10 ms.

\strong{Tokens set aside.} No gaps are read from the time steps of these
tokens. Their gaps from rows still count, and the other tokens are read as
usual.
\itemize{
\item A token where two samples with f0 have the same time: the samples are
not one series of frames. The attribute \code{repeated_time} lists these
tokens.
\item With times written to the step, a token where at least 2 steps, and
more than 1 in 8, are exactly 2 units (0.02): its frames are probably
a little more than one unit apart, as for 12.5 ms frames among 10 ms
ones. A 10 ms token with that many gaps is set aside too, as the two
cannot be told apart. The attribute \code{other_step} lists these tokens.
}

A sample with f0 but no time leaves a hole in the time steps. On a regular
grid its neighbours are then two steps apart, which reads as a gap.

\strong{Gaps an earlier removal marked.} When the data already have a \code{run}
column (\code{voiced_run}, from an earlier \code{\link[=drop_rows_without_f0]{drop_rows_without_f0()}} or from F0
Extraction), a change in its value inside a token is a gap too. This keeps
the gaps from rows removed earlier, which the time steps cannot show on a
grid that is not regular. \code{run = NULL} ignores the column.

\strong{Short edge runs.} A run is a stretch of samples with f0 between two
gaps, or between a gap and the start or end of the token. A run of 1 or 2
samples that a gap cuts off at the start or end of a token is often a
tracking error in the silence. It holds the token's first or last f0, so
it sets where the 0-1 time axes start or end, whether or not the rows
without f0 are removed. \code{frag_start} and \code{frag_end} count these samples:
those before the token's first run of at least 3 samples, and those after
its last. A token with no run that long has none.
\code{drop_rows_without_f0(min_run = 3)} removes them.

\strong{Equal row counts.} When every token has the same number of rows \code{N},
as after resampling to \code{N} points, and a token's first or last row has no
f0, removing that row leaves the token with fewer than \code{N} points. The
time axes that run from the first f0 to the last (\code{token_t01},
\verb{<tier>_t01} and the models' per-token rescale) start or end such a token
one point in whether or not the row is removed, so its points sit off the
\code{N}-point grid, stretched by at least \code{(N - 1) / (N - 2)}, 5\% for 21
points. \code{time_prop} is used as it is and keeps the grid. The attribute
\code{stretch} records this case, and the same for the short edge runs that
\code{min_run = 3} removes, which do move the first or last f0.

\strong{Tokens missing an end point.} A token with f0 whose first or last row
has no f0 is missing an end point. When every token has \code{N} rows, such a
token cannot have \code{N} points over the part with f0. The attribute
\code{edge_missing} lists these tokens, and the summary counts them and their
rows. They are counted for any data, but matter most when every token has
the same number of points, as after resampling.

\strong{Values that may mark no f0.} Some trackers write 0 or a value such as
-1 for unvoiced frames, in a column \code{\link[=zero_hz_to_na]{zero_hz_to_na()}} does not pick. The
attribute \code{unvoiced_suggested} gives the values \code{\link[=suggest_unvoiced]{suggest_unvoiced()}} finds
in the \code{f0} column, and the rows that hold each. It is read from the
column as it is, before \code{unvoiced}, so it stays the same once the values
are passed to \code{unvoiced}.
}
